MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Not from within the GUI, but there is a function to convert a contingency table matrix to a data.frame.

Suppose you have a matrix named cont.matrix, as in the following

> cont.matrix <- matrix(c(4,5,10,4),ncol=2)
> cont.matrix
    [,1] [,2]
[1,]    4   10
[2,]    5    4

then you can convert it using the table.to.data function:

> dat <- table.to.data(cont.matrix)
> dat
  V1 V2
1   1  1
2   1  1
3   1  1
4   1  1
5   1  2
6   1  2
7   1  2
8   1  2
9   1  2
10  1  2
11  1  2
12  1  2
13  1  2
14  1  2
15  2  1
16  2  1
17  2  1
18  2  1
19  2  1
20  2  2
21  2  2
22  2  2
23  2  2

You should now have a data.frame named dat which you can analyze with the contingency table dialog:

> tables<-contingency.tables(
+ row.vars=d(V1),
+ col.vars=d(V2),data=dat)
> tables<-add.chi.squared(tables)
> print(tables,prop.r=T,prop.c=T,prop.t=F)
================================================================================

              ==================================================
                    ========== Table: V1 by V2 ==========
                      | V2
                   V1 |        1  |        2  | Row Total |
-----------------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|
          1  Count    |        4  |       10  |       14  |
             Row %    |   28.571% |   71.429% |   60.870% |
             Column % |   44.444% |   71.429% |           |
-----------------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|
          2  Count    |        5  |        4  |        9  |
             Row %    |   55.556% |   44.444% |   39.130% |
             Column % |   55.556% |   28.571% |           |
-----------------------|-----------|-----------|-----------|
         Column Total |        9  |       14  |       23  |
             Column % |   39.130% |   60.870% |           |

           Large Sample
      Test Statistic    DF p-value | Effect Size est. Lower (%) Upper (%)
Chi Squared 1.675        1  0.196   | Cramer's V  0.27 0 (2.5)   0.678 (97.5)
-----------

================================================================================
> remove(tables)

Ian

